since beginning of the pandemic he's  been teleworking  so he's been doing it from a restored  1976  trailer that he calls his mobile data  science lab  but he lives near ottawa so it's getting  kind of cold in there  so i'm not sure how much longer he's  going to last  everyone please welcome yvonne to the  stage  uh thank you as jared just mentioned i  work for the  department of national defense in canada  and today i'm going to talk to you about  how we are  increasingly using r for advanced  analytics  and also show you some examples of  projects that  my team has done recently  but before i know it was a recurring  team  in some of the previous editions of this  conference  uh but the military and defense  organizations in general  in many nations have a long tradition  of data analytics this is a picture of  dr george lindsay  he's considered to be the father of  military operations research  in canada and he's been leading the  organization  i worked for almost 20 years back in the  60s and the 70s  and just to give you an example of his  work uh  during world war ii george lindsay was  working on predictors  predictors are the fire control systems  that were processing signals from the  first generation of radars  and and using that data to predict the  motion of german aircraft  and shoot at them automatically so  predictors were initially mechanical  systems before before they became  electronic  and that's how uh george lindsay  described them  in a paper he wrote a few years before  he passed  he said today such problems would be  classified as  enter artificial intelligence  and uh he was right i think some people  would call that prescriptive analytics  or automated decision making but in fact  this was done  in the early 1940s is uh you know  pretty impressive another interesting  fact  uh george lindsay is also one of the  fathers of sabermetrics so  in the late 50s he was already  publishing papers on metrics that can be  used to compare  baseball players that's 20 years before  bill james started to publish books on  this  45 years before moneyball so that's also  very  impressive all that to say uh  that data analytics is not exactly new  to the defense community  now of course what constitute data  analytics is something that is  still a little bit debated some of my  colleagues  in dnd see analytics as this  new great big thing others based on  examples like the one i just showed you  are asking well isn't it the same old  thing that we have been doing  for 75 years and my answer to that is  always yes and no  because traditionally when a personal  analyst in d  and d apply their statistical expertise  or their scientific expertise to a  problem  they tend to produce a one-off analysis  that gets to be published in a report  or a paper or a briefing to a client  but data analyst and dnd rarely produce  models that are deployed in our  enterprise systems  to be reused on a regular basis or  models that will transform new data  coming in without having a scientist in  the loop to  process it or integrate it and to me  that's what  modern data science requires that's what  modern data analytics requires being  able to leverage  a fairly large amount of data that we  have in our in our systems develop data  products  that will ultimately live on those  systems  uh very often using cutting-edge tools  and cutting-edge statistical techniques  and that's exactly what my team is doing  you can see us here in a picture taken  before kovid  since then a few more people including  students have joined the team  and i will show you in a minute uh some  of the great work that  that they have been doing recently um  just to be clear  we're not the only data scientist in dnd  and we're not the only scientist in dnd  to do machine learning  but we are the only team to be embedded  with the chief data officers  organization  and we're the only team focused on  developing models  that will ultimately be deployed in our  enterprise systems  for routine decision support we don't  want our models  to be used exclusively by scientists we  want them to feed dashboards and  applications  that will be used in the recurring  fashions by planners decision makers  and that is really uh it's a bit of a  culture shift for us  in dnd um it's also a culture shift from  an i.t standpoint  uh to be really effective at doing that  kind of  enterprise data analytics you need the  right tools and the right infrastructure  and most of the tools that we have been  using historically in dnd for data  analysis  are not very well adapted to modern data  science  so when this team was formed a few years  ago we made the deliberate choice  to use almost exclusively open source  tools  for a number of reasons but i can  summarize these reasons maybe  into one as as wayne gretzky said  one of our great canadian heroes skate  where the puck is going to be  not where it has been so that's true for  aki that's also true for  it as well in the past  most of the data analysis that was done  in dnd was done using commercial tools  like these  but when it comes to developing and  deploying advanced analytics models  these days uh open to open source tools  like  r python spark are really the way to go  and i know i'm preaching to convert it  here but i think it's a message that we  need to keep relaying  within our government organizations it's  not just a question of getting access  to the most cutting-edge tools it's also  a question  of getting the best talent possible and  if you want to recruit  good talent you have to make these tools  available within your organization  and make them easy to use  that's easy to say of course but in  practice it's not so easy  there are often i.t roadblocks as a few  people i've already mentioned in this  conference  what has been a game changer for us in  dmd is getting access to commercial  cloud  access to cloud gave us amazing data  science virtual machines  sandboxes more computing power gitlab  servers to share our code  and also a means to deploy some of our  models into production  and the good news is that in the  government of canada  we have recently adopted a cloud-first  strategy in which cloud  is the preferred option for delivering  it services  so that will hopefully make the life a  lot easier  for all of the all of the data  scientists out there across the  government  okay so let me now show you three  projects  that my team has been working on  recently  i selected projects for which we have  used r  but i also try to select projects that i  think will  resonate the most with other government  departments that are represented  in this conference starting with  some of the work we have done in support  of operation laser  operation laser is the canadian armed  forces response to the pandemic  and a good part of it involves  supporting other government departments  and provincial governments requesting  military assistance  so for example over the last few months  the canadian armed forces  have assisted remote communities in the  north of the country  uh they were also asked to provide  assistance in over 50 long-term care  facilities  in ontario and quebec to do all kinds of  medical support and logical logistical  support tasks  but of course any good military  operation starts with  good planning and good intelligence and  at the very early stages of the pandemic  our medical intelligence staff  was already trying to predict how the  pandemic would progress  and where requests from requests for  assistance would  come from and although there are quite a  lot of models and dashboards available  to do that these days  uh back in february march there was not  that many tools available to provide  that kind of information  uh beyond maybe the dashboard from uh  john hopkins university  so what talia beach and my team  did is to produce this animated map of  the virus progression over time and  space  and this was done using r and using open  source data collected by the university  of toronto  open source shape files with postal code  areas from  statistics canada and back in april this  was probably the most geographically  detailed picture  of how the virus was progressing in  canada  so that map became very popular very  quickly within dnd  and even with other government  departments and it has informed some of  the planning  decisions related to a laser  initially the map was just an animated  gif uh  but a few weeks after we released italia  embedded the map into an r shiny app  which enabled users to have more control  on it picking an exact date  zooming in zooming in on a specific  region  and not only that italia added some  predictive component to the map so the  users can get an estimate of the number  of new cases  in each region up to seven days into the  future  so that's that's that was a pretty cool  project  my second project that i want to show  you is about  march madness unfortunately it's not  about basketball  it's about finance because you have to  note in the government of canada  fiscal years are ending in march and at  the end of the fiscal year  uh is when a lot of contracts with the  government are ending  uh that's when a lot of goods and  equipments are received  so it's not uncommon to see managers  spend up to 20  or even 30 of their annual budgets  during the very last month of the year  and that's why we call this march  madness  but often managers cannot spend all of  their remaining budget in march  because a contract may have been delayed  or because a piece of equipment  has not been delivered on time and when  that happens  it's often too late to reallocate the  money elsewhere  so in an ideal world you would like to  predict early in the fiscal year  say after just five of five or six  months  which managers will understand which  managers  may actually require more money and  reallocate your resources accordingly  problem is uh it's not that easy to  predict how much money managers will  spend  uh early in the fiscal year so what we  have done  here is try to leverage the huge amount  of historical financial data that we  have  in dnd to better understand some of our  spending patterns  and use these patterns to make  predictions  uh there are probably different  techniques to do that like time series  analysis for example  uh but the method we we initially looked  at  is to build a regression model that  predicts the amount spent  at the end of the year based on a number  of potential variables for example if  you plot  uh how much a certain manager has spent  at the end of the fiscal year against  how much he has spent  after only five months uh you see that  you know  there's clearly a relationship between  these two things over the years  and and you can exploit that  relationship you can do the same thing  with other variables how much money was  committed how much money was allocated  and you can build a  pretty good linear model out of that  which is exactly what uh cheri  gunasekara in my team  and he's on at this conference that's  what he has done  he built a regression model that he  implemented into a shiny app  um right now this shiny app is not  directly connected to our financial  system but it can take data extracts  from our financial system  and generate pretty good forecasts uh in  red there with the 95  95 confidence interval uh fairly early  in the fiscal year so this is just a  prototype  uh but we are working with our financial  branch to test it on different funds  with a view of deploying that kind of  statistical model  directly into our financial systems in  the future  my last example about the is about the  public service  employee survey and this is a survey  conducted every year  in canada to get the views of all the  employees of the federal government  about their workplaces so it's a fairly  comprehensive survey with over 180  questions  that covers different teams like  employee engagement  leadership workplace compensation and so  on  and the goal of the survey is really to  allow the government departments to  identify  what they're doing well and what they're  doing not so well  in terms of managing their people so all  the departments  have a close look at these results  uh every year and they develop action  plans to address issues  that are raised in this survey which is  good  but most departments at the moment are  still analyzing the results  in a very traditional fashion uh so for  example they build dashboards and they  look at where they're scoring high where  they're scoring low  how much their scores have changed since  the last survey  and obviously that's a natural place to  start  um but it's not necessarily the optimal  way of exploiting the data  uh because this day this survey has a  lot of questions  so it's often hard for the departments  or the department branches to figure out  what part of the results they really  need to focus on  um so one approach to that problem that  we have successfully  used in my branch of dnd is to do peer  comparisons  so for example if you take the score  obtained by a branch to a specific  question  and you compare it to those of all the  other branches in the government you can  quickly find uh if you are in the bottom  10  or in the top 10 percent of all the  branches  so that's a very simple approach uh but  it tells you  very quickly uh the kind of results that  you should be have a  closer look at and we have done  dashboards  to allow departments to do that kind of  analysis very quickly  but even with that kind of tools the  results of the survey are not always  easy to interpret or address because of  how certain questions are framed  for example one question of the survey  is  i am satisfied with my department and  people have to answer  on a numerical scale to what extent we  agree or disagree with that statement  so if a department gets a low score on  that question  uh how can you interpret that you know  how can you explain  what causes employee dissatisfaction and  more importantly  what can you do about it so  that's something we've tried to to  answer  as part of a project we did in  collaboration with the national research  council  we developed a new set of dashboards  using r  that first have a look at how the survey  responses are correlated with each other  so in this case we use a forced layout  algorithm in  r to visualize the correlation matrix  between the responses  and that allows us to spot very quickly  what are the  really central questions of the survey  for example question 44 i am satisfied  with my department  um that's right in the middle there  question 66  my workplace is psychologically healthy  question 26 i  am satisfied with the quality of the  supervision i receive  these questions are strongly correlated  to a lot of other questions in the  survey  and you cannot just look at them in  isolation  what you can do or however is to you  know if you get a low score to this  question  you can try to understand what are the  main influencers and  again there are means to do that in r  you can build a  causal graph that estimates the strength  and the direction  of the influence between different  questions so for example  in q66 my places my workplace is  psychologically healthy  uh you can see that's influenced by  question 65 responses  about awareness and mental health it's  influence  by also responses to question uh four  i have support to at work to balance my  work and my personal life or  question 54 my department works hard to  create a workplace that prevents  harassment  that's really interesting because it's a  little bit more clear  and it's more actionable i'm going to  give you another example that is even  more telling uh you can use a machine  learning approach  to do the similar kind of analysis if  you look at  question 26 i am satisfied with this  quality of the supervision i receive uh  which is a very central question in the  survey  you can train a random forest model to  determine what are the top predictors  of responses to that question and if you  look at the results it tells you that  the most important predictors  are the responses to q22 i receive  useful  feedback from my supervisor question 24  my supervisor keeps me informed about  issues affecting my work  question six i receive meaningful  recognition for work well done  and so on so again my point here is that  this kind of insight is useful  and it's sectionable and it can inform  action plans that will ultimately  improve the the general satisfaction of  employees  and we again we we develop dashboards in  r  and also using a combination of r and  power bi that make that kind of analysis  very easy and very quick to do  um so just before i finish very briefly  i want to mention  some of the other projects my team has  been doing recently using r  for example we've done work with  the royal navy about personnel  enrollment and selection  modeling uh we've done work with our  material branch on procurement cost  forecasting  uh we're doing some work with our nato  allies including the us  on predictive maintenance and also uh  we did some work recently on supply  chain forecasting and a bunch of other  exciting things  in other languages like like python and  and  and commercial tools but uh you know  that  there's a lot to be done in in data  science and we're not uh  we don't have any kind of shortage of  problems that we can work with  so on that note um i'd like to  thank you again thank you jared for the  invitation and uh  have a good day i love that we got to  see the trailer at the end there as if  the  statistics wasn't good enough we got to  see his trailer so that was uh that was  pretty awesome  okay as promised we are now going to do  the uh

